    {"n":311,"term":"Concrete slab thickness to a floor load (psf)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["slab load","slab thickness to psf","concrete slab weight","floor load from slab","150 pcf times thickness over 12","concrete weight to floor load","convert concrete weight to floor load","slab dead load","5 in slab psf","8 in slab psf","change it to a floor load"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Floor load = 150 pounds per cubic foot x 2 inches /12 inches per foot = 25 pounds per square foot.","date":"2026-09-23","src":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 29 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"},{"quote":"And then the concrete weighs 150 pounds per cubic foot. So 62.5 pounds per square foot.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 29 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"On a set of drawings, framing plan, midterm and final exam, you might be given the weight of concrete. You have to remember to change it to a floor load.","date":"2026-09-23","src":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 35 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"}],"notes":["5 in slab -> 5/12 x 150 = 62.5 psf (9/30); 2 in -> 25 psf, 8 in -> 100 psf (9/23)"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 29 (CART, class 2026-09-23); transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 29 (CART, class 2026-09-30); transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 35 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"},
    {"n":312,"term":"Steel and wood framing weight is given as a psf number","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["steel framing weight","framing weight psf","steel framing allowance","weight of the steel framing","framing weight given in psf","steel allowance","self weight of framing on the midterm"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"On the midterm and dreaded design project, there will be a floor number on pounds per square foot.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 29 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"The only difference is that you will be given the steel framing as a pound per square foot number so you don't have to go back and forth.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"The interesting thing about steel and wood design is we take steel framing and wood framing as a pound per square number.","date":"2026-09-23","src":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 35 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"}],"notes":["homework gives no framing allowance: choose a size, add the self weight, check again (entry \"Self-weight: choose a size, add it, check again\")"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 29 (CART, class 2026-09-30); transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30); transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 35 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"},
    {"n":313,"term":"Self-weight: choose a size, add it, check again","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["self weight of a beam","beam self weight","add the self weight","self-weight check","self weight added after choosing","choose a size and add the self weight"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We'll choose a size and add the self weight. And see if it still works.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 33 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"Unless there's a very good reason to pick something that isn't economical.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"}],"notes":["second quote is only the end of her sentence about economy; see entry \"Most economical = the lightest, not the closest\""],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 33 (CART, class 2026-09-30); transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},
    {"n":314,"term":"Most economical = the lightest, not the closest","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["most economical","most economical beam","lightest not closest","boldface economy table","which beam is most economical","economical section","cheapest section","lightest section"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"We are not looking for the one that's closest. We are looking for the lightest.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"They are grouped into bunches of similar section modular and the moment capacity and the one in boldface is the most economical.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"we want to pick the most economical to compare weight because it takes out length and fabrication and if we compare weight, and the lighter it is the cheeper it is.","date":"2026-08-26","src":"transcripts/2026-08-26 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 41 (CART, class 2026-08-26)"}],"notes":["CART garble: 'section modular' = section modulus; see also the entry 'Economy table (Table 3-2)'"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30); transcripts/2026-08-26 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 41 (CART, class 2026-08-26)"},
    {"n":315,"term":"Bolt hole area is a rectangle, not a circle","topic":"Tension members","kind":"definition","synonyms":["bolt hole area","rectangle not a circle","area lost to a bolt hole","hole is round but the area is a rectangle","why a rectangle for bolt holes","area removed by a hole","rectangle for each hole","net area rectangle"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Pointing out the bolt hole is round the area we subject is a rectangle. It's the whole diameter plus an eighth of an inch.","date":"2026-09-02","src":"transcripts/2026-09-02 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 29 (CART, class 2026-09-02)"},{"quote":"The area which was the Geometric Property that resists tension is reduced by two rectangles; not by 8 circles.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 47 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"}],"notes":["CART garble: \"Pointing out\" = probably \"Pointing out that\"; the 9/30 example is a W12x65 with two holes in the web (2 rectangles of 1 in x 0.39 in)"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-02 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 29 (CART, class 2026-09-02); transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 47 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},
    {"n":316,"term":"Minimum two bolts","topic":"Tension members","kind":"definition","synonyms":["minimum two bolts","minimum number of bolts","at least two bolts","one bolt","only need one bolt","if the one bolt fails the whole connection fails","how many bolts minimum"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"Even if our calculations say we only need one bolt we put it for it. If the one bolt fails, the whole connection fails.","date":"2026-09-02","src":"transcripts/2026-09-02 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 53 (CART, class 2026-09-02)"},{"quote":"Do Not use 2 or less fasteners","date":null,"src":"quiz1/wk2-tension-lecture.md line 340 (her slide wk2 p.12 (for the shear lag factor of angles))"}],"notes":["CART garble: \"we put it for it\" is probably \"we put two in\"; the second quote is about U for angles (Case 8: with fewer than three fasteners per line use Case 2)"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-02 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 53 (CART, class 2026-09-02); quiz1/wk2-tension-lecture.md line 340 (her slide wk2 p.12 (for the shear lag factor of angles))"},
    {"n":317,"term":"Compression capacity uses the gross area (no net area)","topic":"Compression members","kind":"definition","synonyms":["compression uses gross area","why gross area for compression","no a net in compression","no net area in compression","holes in compression members","bolt holes in a column","why only one equation for compression","column area gross or net","bolt holes in compression","holes filled by bolts"],"not":["tension rupture"],"definitions":[{"quote":"There's no A net here because even if there are holes to make a connection between pieces in a compression system, the shaft of the bolts are made of material that is stronger with the base material and just becomes part of the air way.","date":"2026-09-09","src":"transcripts/2026-09-09 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-09)"}],"notes":["CART garble: \"the air way\" = probably \"the area\"; \"five times F critical\" = phi Fcr"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-09 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-09)"},
    {"n":318,"term":"Load path: slab to beam to girder to column","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["load path","slab beam girder column","how does load flow","load flows from slab to beams to girders to columns","path of the load","framing hierarchy","slab load gets to the beam"],"not":[],"definitions":[{"quote":"The slab load gets in there, but it gets in there through the beam.","date":"2026-09-23","src":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 35 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"},{"quote":"And they deposit a point load on diverters. So when we look at the girders they are just going to have one point load on them equal to two become rep actions.","date":"2026-09-23","src":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 35 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"},{"quote":"The slab bends in response to the load, load to the beams the beams responds to the point loads delivered by the beams.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"}],"notes":["CART garbles: \"diverters\" = girders; \"two become rep actions\" = two beam reactions"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 35 (CART, class 2026-09-23); transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},
    {"n":319,"term":"Maximum moment: uniform load wL^2/8 (Table 3-22 diagram 1)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["wl^2/8","wl squared over 8","maximum moment uniform load","mmax uniform","moment for a uniform load","diagram 1","moment of a simply supported beam with uniform load","w l squared over eight","mu = wu l^2/8"],"not":["point load at midspan","pl/4"],"definitions":[{"quote":"WL squared over 8.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"diagram 1 shows a uniform load and the maximum moment happens at midspan","date":"2026-09-23","src":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 31 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"},{"quote":"For designing beams, you want to go to the beam chapter.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 33 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"}],"notes":["9/30 example: wu 1.62 kip/ft, 30 ft -> 1.62 x 30^2 / 8 = 182.7 kip-ft","CART garble in 9/23: \"WL /square root of 8\" = wL^2/8"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30); transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 31 (CART, class 2026-09-23); transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 33 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},
    {"n":320,"term":"Maximum moment: single point load at midspan PL/4 (diagram 7)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["pl/4","p l over 4","maximum moment point load at center","point load at midspan moment","diagram 7","moment of a single load in the center of the span","mu = pu l / 4","single point load moment","moment girder one beam at midspan"],"not":["wl^2/8","uniform load"],"definitions":[{"quote":"Somebody tell me what the equation is for the maximum moment or a single load in the center of the span?","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 47 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"Correction: Mu=P uL/ 4 You are looking at diagram 7.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 47 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},{"quote":"You will be asked to calculate moments on the midterm. You'll only be using the economy table, but you have to be familiar with the moment equation.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 47 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"}],"notes":["9/30 example: girder 20 ft with a 62 kip point load at midspan -> 62 x 20 / 4 = 310 kip-ft"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 47 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},
    {"n":321,"term":"Maximum moment: two equal point loads P a (+ wL^2/8 for self-weight)","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["p a","pa moment","two point loads moment","moment from two point loads","maximum moment pa","mu = pa","point loads at the third points","girder with two beams moment","moment with self weight and point loads"],"not":["pl/4"],"definitions":[{"quote":"The maximum moment is Pa. Since the maximums happening in the same place, I can just add them. M max equals to W L squared over 8 plus Pa.","date":"2026-09-23","src":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 31 (CART, class 2026-09-23)"},{"quote":"Mu equals Pa 49. 84 (8)= 398. 7 kips.","date":"2026-09-30","src":"transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"}],"notes":["9/30 example: two 49.84 kip beam reactions a = 8 ft from each end of a 24 ft girder -> 398.7 kip-ft; CART garble: \"WL /square root of 8\" in the surrounding sentence = wL^2/8"],"source":"transcripts/2026-09-23 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 31 (CART, class 2026-09-23); transcripts/2026-09-30 -- ARCH-232 session.md line 37 (CART, class 2026-09-30)"},
    {"n":322,"term":"Table 1-1 (W-shapes: dimensions and properties)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"definition","synonyms":["table 1-1","table 1-1 w-shapes","w-shapes dimensions","w shape properties table","where are w shape properties","dimensions and properties of w shapes","what is table 1-1"],"not":["table 1-7"],"definitions":[{"quote":"Table 1-1 (continued) - W-Shapes, Dimensions","date":null,"src":"quiz1/wk1-intro-lecture.md line 172 (her slide p.6)"},{"quote":"W-Shapes Dimensions","date":null,"src":"quiz1/wk2-tension-lecture.md line 352 (her slide wk2 p.13 (Table 1-1 printed on her slide))"}],"notes":["her slide p.6 uses the W27x84 row of Table 1-1 to show how a designation is read; the properties (I, S, r, Z) are on the facing page"],"source":"quiz1/wk1-intro-lecture.md line 172 (her slide p.6); quiz1/wk2-tension-lecture.md line 352 (her slide wk2 p.13 (Table 1-1 printed on her slide))"},
    {"n":323,"term":"Table 1-7 (angles: properties)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"definition","synonyms":["table 1-7","table 1-7 angles","angles properties","angle properties table","where are angle properties","table 1-7a","workable gages in angle legs","what is table 1-7"],"not":["table 1-1","table 2-4"],"definitions":[{"quote":"Table 1-7, Angles, Properties","date":null,"src":"quiz1/wk2-tension-lecture.md line 155 (her slide wk2 p.5)"}],"notes":["her slide wk2 p.5 uses the L8x6x3/4 row (A = 9.99 in^2) of the angle table for the double-angle tension example; the workable gages of the angle legs are the companion Table 1-7A (not on her slides)"],"source":"quiz1/wk2-tension-lecture.md line 155 (her slide wk2 p.5)"},
    {"n":324,"term":"Table 4-1a (column loads, W-shapes, Fy = 50 ksi)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"definition","synonyms":["table 4-1a","table 4-1 w-shapes","available strength in axial compression w-shapes","column table w shapes","where is column capacity for w shapes","what is table 4-1a"],"not":["table 4-14","table 4-5"],"definitions":[{"quote":"Table 4-1a - Available Strength in Axial Compression, kips, W-Shapes, W14, F_y = 50 ksi","date":null,"src":"quiz1/wk3-4-compression-lecture.md line 639 (her slide wk4 p.3)"}],"notes":["her class wording is the entry 'Column load tables (Table 4-1)'; the tables work only when KxLx = KyLy"],"source":"quiz1/wk3-4-compression-lecture.md line 639 (her slide wk4 p.3)"},
    {"n":325,"term":"Table 4-5 (column loads, round HSS)","topic":"The Manual","kind":"definition","synonyms":["table 4-5","table 4-5 round hss","round hss column table","available strength in axial compression round hss","where is round hss column capacity","what is table 4-5"],"not":["table 4-1a","table 4-14"],"definitions":[{"quote":"Available Strength in Axial Compression, kips, Round HSS","date":null,"src":"quiz1/wk3-4-compression-lecture.md line 805 (her slide wk4 p.8)"}],"notes":["used for HW 5-8 (HSS6.000x0.188, KL = 21 ft, phi Pn = 48.0 kips); her class: \"I go to the table that has rounds\""],"source":"quiz1/wk3-4-compression-lecture.md line 805 (her slide wk4 p.8)"},
    {"n":326,"term":"Shape factor","topic":"Beams and floor framing","kind":"definition","synonyms":["shape factor","what is the shape factor","define shape factor","shape factor of a section","shape factor for a rectangle","shape factor of a rectangle","shape factor mp/my","mp/my","mp over my","z/s","z over s","plastic moment over yield moment","ratio of the plastic moment to the yield moment"],"not":["plastic section modulus","elastic section modulus","section modulus"],"definitions":[{"quote":"Shape factor = Mp/My = Z/S = (bd^2/4)/(bd^2/6) = 1.5 For a Rectangle","date":null,"src":"Materials/Steel 5 Beam Design IN PERSON CLASS  16th ed  Canvas.pdf, page 3 (her handwritten slide, Week 5 Beam Design; read from the page image, the PDF has no text layer for it)"}],"notes":["her slide is handwritten: the quote is read from the page image, and her stacked fractions are written on one line (the page has Sx = bd^2/6 and Z = bd^2/4 just above it: S is the elastic and Z the plastic section modulus of the rectangle b x d, My = Fy S, Mp = Fy Z)","a solid round bar is not on her slide: Z/S = (d^3/6) / (pi d^3/32) = 16 / (3 pi) = 1.698","the tool works these out in Lookup > Section properties (a rectangle gives S, Z and 1.5; a round bar gives S, Z and 1.698)","see also 'Plastic section modulus Z', 'Elastic section modulus S' and 'Plastic moment Mp (AISC glossary)'"],"source":"Materials/Steel 5 Beam Design IN PERSON CLASS  16th ed  Canvas.pdf, page 3 (her handwritten slide; page image gauntlet/authority2/pages/steel5_p03.png)"}
